An analysis of the issue indicates that the test setup function is executed only once per test suite. However, some test suites contain a mix of tests running in kernel mode (`ZTEST`) and user mode (`ZTEST_USER`). When user mode is enabled, these tests run in different threads. The setup function grants necessary kernel object permissions to the thread that is currently running. If the setup runs only once, these permissions are granted only to the thread of the first test case, causing subsequent tests in different execution contexts (e.g., a user mode test after a kernel mode test) to fail due to lack of permissions, leading to a segmentation fault when accessing kernel objects.

The fix is to move the test setup logic from the one-time `setup` function to the per-test `before` function for test suites that mix kernel and user mode tests. This ensures that permissions are correctly granted to the appropriate thread before each test execution. This change is required for the `can_classic` and `canfd` test suites.

